/-! # Composition of blowups with controlled centres and strict transforms

Source: Stacks Project, Spaces Divisors, Tags 0860 and 0868, with Tag 0863
(blueprint: lem-sfb-space-composition-centres-and-transforms).

Blowups and strict transforms are specified up to their unique isomorphisms preserving
all structure maps. Compatibility with both projections is compatibility with the
closed inclusions into the same fibre product.
-/

@[expose] public noncomputable section

set_option autoImplicit false
set_option backward.isDefEq.respectTransparency false

universe u

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits

namespace AlgebraicGeometry.AlgebraicSpace

/-- The underlying centre zero locus, computed on etale scheme tests.
Source: Stacks Project, Spaces Divisors, Tag 085Q, centre of a blowing up
(blueprint: lem-sfb-space-composition-centres-and-transforms). -/
def idealZeroLocus {S : Scheme.{u}} {X : AlgebraicSpace S}
    (I : QuasiCoherentIdeal X) : Set (Point X.obj.obj) :=
  {x | ∃ (V : X.EtaleSite) (y : X.etaleForget.obj V),
    y ∈ (blowupChartIdeal I V).support ∧ schemePointMap V.obj.hom y = x}

/-- Two finite-type blowups over a qcqs space compose to a finite-type blowup, with
exactly the union centre support and the canonical comparison of all strict transforms.
Source: Stacks Project, Spaces Divisors, Tags 0860(1)-(2), 0868(2), and 0863
(blueprint: lem-sfb-space-composition-centres-and-transforms).

Proof:
1. Apply [lem-sfb-space-blowup-finite-degree-ideal-descent] to $\mathcal I_0$ and $\mathcal J$.
   The hypotheses are precisely qcqs $T$ and finite-type quasi-coherent ideals on $T$ and $T_1$.
   Obtain $d>0$ and $\mathcal H\subseteq\mathcal I_0^d$ of finite type such that $\mathcal
   H\mathcal O_{T_1}=\mathcal E^d\mathcal J$, where $\mathcal E=\mathcal I_0\mathcal O_{T_1}$.
   By [lem-sfb-space-blowup-chart-properties], $\mathcal E$ is an effective Cartier ideal.
2. Put $R=\operatorname{Bl}_{\mathcal H\mathcal O_{T_1}}T_1$. There are inverse canonical maps
   $R\leftrightarrows T_2$ over $T_1$, as follows. On $T_2$, the ideals $\mathcal J\mathcal
   O_{T_2}$ and $\mathcal E\mathcal O_{T_2}$ are effective Cartier: the first by [lem-sfb-space-
   blowup-chart-properties], the second by [lemma-nagata-cartier-total-transform-blowup] on
   scheme charts. Hence their product $\mathcal E^d\mathcal J\mathcal O_{T_2}$ is effective
   Cartier (locally a product of non-zero-divisors). The universal property [lem-sfb-space-
   blowup-universal-property] gives $T_2\to R$. Conversely on $R$, $\mathcal E^d\mathcal
   J\mathcal O_R$ is effective Cartier by the same chart-properties node. On each scheme chart,
   [lemma-nagata-invertible-product-factor] applied to its factors gives that $\mathcal
   J\mathcal O_R$ is effective Cartier, yielding $R\to T_2$ by the universal property. Both
   composites are identities by the uniqueness clause of that property, since their composites
   to $T_1$ are the structure maps. The use of the two scheme lemmas is legitimate: the chart-
   properties node identifies these blowups after etale base change with scheme blowups; Cartier
   regularity is etale local by its definition.
3. Set $\mathcal L=\mathcal I_0\mathcal H$. This is quasi-coherent and finite type: on each
   affine etale chart the product is generated by the finitely many pairwise products of
   generators, and [lem-sfb-space-qcoh-affine-chart-calculus] computes images and tensor
   products on charts. Apply [lem-sfb-space-blowup-product-ideal-comparison] to $\mathcal
   I_0,\mathcal H$. Its second blowup is exactly $R$, so its canonical isomorphism, composed
   with the preceding $T_2\cong R$, is the required $e:T_2\cong\operatorname{Bl}_{\mathcal L}T$
   over $T$.
4. Because $\mathcal H\subseteq\mathcal I_0^d$ and $d>0$, $V(\mathcal I_0)\subseteq V(\mathcal
   H)$. Off $V(\mathcal I_0)$, [lem-sfb-space-blowup-chart-properties] identifies $p$ with the
   identity and $\mathcal E$ with the unit ideal. The equality $\mathcal H\mathcal
   O_{T_1}=\mathcal E^d\mathcal J$ therefore identifies $V(\mathcal H)$ on this open with
   $V(\mathcal J)$ on its inverse image. Consequently $|V(\mathcal H)|=|V(\mathcal
   I_0)|\cup|p|(|V(\mathcal J)|)$. Finally $V(\mathcal I_0\mathcal H)=V(\mathcal I_0)\cup
   V(\mathcal H)$, checked at a prime on a scheme chart, proves the required support equality
   for $\mathcal L$.
5. It remains to compare the two closed subspaces of $Y\times_TT_2$. Choose a surjective etale
   scheme atlas $V\to T$, then a surjective etale scheme atlas $U\to Y\times_TV$. These give a
   jointly surjective etale cover after every fibre product, by [lem-trf-space-fibre-products-
   etale-covers]. By [lem-sfb-space-blowup-chart-properties], both blowups over $V$ are ordinary
   scheme blowups, and $e$ base changes to their composite-blowup isomorphism. The support
   equality over $V$ follows by repeating step 4 for the restricted ideals: $\mathcal
   H|_V\subseteq(\mathcal I_0|_V)^d$, the equality on the first blowup restricts to the same
   equality, and that blowup is the identity off its centre. Apply [lem-sfb-space-strict-
   transform-etale-locality] first to $p$, then to $q$, and also to the blowup of $\mathcal L$.
   It identifies the restrictions of the successive transforms with the successive scheme
   transforms of $U\to V$, and identifies the restriction of the single transform with its
   scheme transform. On schemes these torsion-convention transforms are those of [def-nagata-
   strict-transform]: a section is supported on $V(a)$, $a$ a local exceptional equation, iff a
   power of $a$ kills it, and [lem-trf-strict-transform-sheaf-kernel] identifies the supported-
   section ideal with the closure transform's ideal. So [lem-trf-strict-transform-composition]
   applies to these schemes, with the pulled-back finite-type ideals and the pulled-back support
   equality, and identifies the two closed subschemes with their inclusions into
   $U\times_V(T_2\times_TV)$. Hence their defining ideal sheaves agree on this etale cover. By
   the closed-subspace/ideal correspondence [lem-sfb-space-ideal-closed-subspace-
   correspondence], they agree globally. The resulting canonical isomorphism is the identity of
   this common closed subspace, so it respects the inclusions and both projections. -/
theorem exists_blowupPresentation_comp_centres_strictTransforms
    {S : Scheme.{u}} (T : AlgebraicSpace S)
    (hqc : ∃ (V : Over S) (a : yoneda.obj V ⟶ T.obj.obj),
      CompactSpace V.left ∧ IsEtale a ∧ IsSurjective a)
    (hqs : IsAbsolutelyQuasiSeparated T)
    (I : QuasiCoherentIdeal T) (hI : (I.val : T.Modules).IsFiniteType)
    (B : BlowupPresentation I)
    (J : QuasiCoherentIdeal B.space) (hJ : (J.val : B.space.Modules).IsFiniteType)
    (C : BlowupPresentation J) :
    ∃ (L : QuasiCoherentIdeal T), (L.val : T.Modules).IsFiniteType ∧
      ∃ (D : BlowupPresentation L) (e : C.space ≅ D.space),
        e.hom ≫ D.projection = C.projection ≫ B.projection ∧
        idealZeroLocus L = idealZeroLocus I ∪
          mapPoint B.projection.hom.hom '' idealZeroLocus J ∧
        ∀ (Y : AlgebraicSpace S) (f : Y ⟶ T)
          (A₁ : StrictTransformPresentation f I B)
          (A₂ : StrictTransformPresentation (A₁.inclusion ≫ A₁.toBlowup) J C)
          (A : StrictTransformPresentation f L D),
          ∃! φ : A₂.space ≅ A.space,
            φ.hom ≫ A.inclusion ≫ A.toSource =
              A₂.inclusion ≫ A₂.toSource ≫ A₁.inclusion ≫ A₁.toSource ∧
            φ.hom ≫ A.inclusion ≫ A.toBlowup = A₂.inclusion ≫ A₂.toBlowup ≫ e.hom := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry.AlgebraicSpace
